 The second method is dependency-closed argument graph repair driven by multiple candidates. It leaves the input text unchanged and combines compatible component-set replacement with reconstruction of all affected relations. Each proposed transaction is accepted or rolled back as a whole under complete-object scores, global structural constraints, and explicit editing budgets. Set replacement represents insertion, deletion, span or type changes, splitting, and merging. Conditional analysis characterizes local utility differences and bounded execution without guaranteeing improvement in true extraction F1.
 
-The target evaluation corpora are AAEC and CDCP, with CDCP multi-premise relations retained as native hyperedges. The specification defines center policies, frozen candidates, state-specific queues, sequential commits, and oracle scope. Finite constructed inputs show that a newly legal relation may require a later transaction and that sequential commits can reduce total surrogate utility. Coordinate-based split and merge fixtures and budget ledgers further delimit actions and failure paths. These execution records check the specification and its boundaries; they do not establish corpus performance. Training gains, the incremental benefit of joint repair, and interaction between the two stages remain unsupported by completed multi-seed corpus experiments.
+Under the AAEC and CDCP task representations, T1 improves strict relation F1 over T0 by 7.32 and 7.21 percentage points, respectively, while BR-J adds 5.99 and 6.71 points over the T1 initial predictions. Comparisons on identical augmentation sets separate the effects of auxiliary supervision and paired alignment. The sequential baseline and joint strategy differ in information use, acceptance conditions, and proposal ranking; their contrast does not isolate execution order alone. The full combination improves on the common starting point by 13.31 and 13.92 points, but the relation interaction intervals include zero. Candidate limitations, long source sets, and harmful edits remain relevant, and local repair trades quality and computation against global optimization.
 
